{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Teaching in Tech-Rich Classrooms With TeachGen@i","title":"Comparing AI Suggestions to Pedagogy","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting comparing ai suggestions to pedagogy to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"02","section":"04","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat531-supplement-02-04-lo-01","statement":"Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-02-04-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-02-04-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-02-04-lo-04","statement":"Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible"}],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i","cat531_supplement_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/02/03"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_2_4_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_2_4_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_2_4_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_2_4_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":4,"top_tier_hardened":false,"lo_practice_map":{"cat531-supplement-02-04-lo-01":["cat531_supplement_2_4_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy_calibration"],"cat531-supplement-02-04-lo-02":["cat531_supplement_2_4_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_2_4_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy_artifact_signal"],"cat531-supplement-02-04-lo-03":["cat531_supplement_2_4_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy_calibration","cat531_supplement_2_4_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy_ai_boundary"],"cat531-supplement-02-04-lo-04":["cat531_supplement_2_4_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy_calibration"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Comparing AI Suggestions to Pedagogy\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Comparing AI Suggestions To Pedagogy, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/02-04-comparing-ai-suggestions-to-pedagogy.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Comparing AI Suggestions to Pedagogy: Comparing AI Suggestions To Pedagogy, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nAn AI suggestion arrives polished, confident, and often genuinely appealing. None of those qualities tell you whether it teaches the thing you intended to teach. The danger is alignment drift: a suggestion that is engaging and well-written but pointed at a cognitive demand one or two levels below your objective. A \"match the equivalent fractions\" race feels like a fractions lesson, yet matching is recognition while your objective asked students to explain. The tool optimized for fluency and fun; it did not optimize for your verb. This section gives you a test that holds any AI suggestion against the actual cognitive demand of your learning objective, so that appeal never substitutes for alignment.\n\nThe artifact, a Design Tension Studio map, records the live tension between a suggestion's appeal and its fit, and the negotiated decision that keeps what is useful while correcting what is misaligned. A reviewer should see that you measured the suggestion against the objective rather than against your gut.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA teacher's objective for the day is precise: \"Students will explain why two fractions are equivalent.\" TeachGen@i offers a sleek opener, a timed escape room where teams race to match cards showing equivalent fractions, first team out wins. It is the kind of activity students cheer for. But the teacher reads the objective's verb again. Explain is a reasoning task; the escape room asks students to recognize matches quickly. A child can win the race without ever saying why one half equals two fourths. The suggestion is not bad; it is aimed at the wrong cognitive level. The decision is what to do with an appealing activity that misses the target by one rung.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nComparing a suggestion to pedagogy means treating the objective's verb as a fixed target and asking what cognitive demand the suggestion actually requires of a student. The two often diverge, and the gap is usually downward: appealing activities tend to live at recognition and recall because those are easy to gamify.\n\n| Objective verb | Cognitive demand it sets | A suggestion that only reaches recognition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Explain / justify | Produce reasoning a listener can follow | Matching cards, multiple choice, a sorting race |\n| Compare / contrast | Articulate a relationship between cases | Highlighting differences without stating why they matter |\n| Apply / solve | Use a procedure in a new situation | Re-watching a worked example, filling a template |\n\nThe test is a single question asked of every suggestion: what is the smallest thing a student could do to \"succeed\" at this activity, and does that smallest thing match my verb? If a student can win the escape room by pattern-matching without reasoning, the activity's true demand is recognition, whatever the packaging suggests. [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) frames this as the content-pedagogy fit: the technological activity must serve the specific content reasoning, not just animate the room. The negotiated decision is rarely \"reject\"; more often it is \"keep the appeal, add the missing rung,\" because a small modification can lift a recognition task to the reasoning the objective requires.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Your objective uses the verb 'explain.' An AI suggests a timed card-matching race for equivalent fractions. Apply the test: what is the smallest thing a student could do to win?\" explanation=\"A student can win the race by pattern-matching cards quickly, never saying why one half equals two fourths. The activity's true floor is recognition under time pressure, which falls short of the 'explain' (reasoning) verb. The gap is one rung, and the fix is to add a reasoning step, not to lower the objective.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Match cards fast without giving any reason, so the true demand is recognition\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Explain the reasoning to a partner, so it already meets the objective\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Nothing, because a fun activity automatically reaches any objective\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nWalk through one annotated judgment call on comparing an AI suggestion to pedagogy. A teacher's objective is: \"Students will explain why two fractions are equivalent.\" TeachGen@i offers a confident suggestion.\n\n**Step 1. State the objective as the test.** The verb is explain, not match or recall. Any suggestion must move students toward producing an explanation.\n\n**Step 2. Hold the suggestion against the objective.**\n\n> **AI suggestion:** \"Open with a fast-paced escape room: students race to match cards showing equivalent fractions; the first team out wins.\" Reads polished and engaging.\n\n> **Pedagogy check (annotated):** Matching cards quickly is recognition under time pressure. It never asks a student to say why one half equals two fourths. Fluent and fun, but off the objective.\n\n**Step 3. Make the negotiated decision on the map.** The teacher does not discard the engagement, and does not accept the activity as-is. Decision recorded: \"Keep the card sort, but after each match students write one sentence using the stem 'These are equivalent because...' and a partner checks it. Rejected the timed-race framing because the clock pushes guessing over reasoning.\"\n\n**Step 4. Check the margins.** A third of the class reads below the card text, so the teacher adds an icon model alongside the words. The map now shows the tension (appeal vs. fit to the explain objective), the negotiated revision, the rejected element with its reason, and the learner-variability adjustment, all auditable by a mentor.\n\nMatch each objective verb to the recognition-level activity that falls short of it. Seeing the mismatch is the alignment test in miniature.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Explain why two fractions are equivalent\",\"definition\":\"A timed card-matching race that rewards fast pattern-matching\"},{\"term\":\"Evaluate whether a primary source is reliable\",\"definition\":\"A drag-to-order timeline that only sequences dates\"},{\"term\":\"Revise a draft for clarity\",\"definition\":\"An AI tool that rewrites the paragraph so the student only accepts\"},{\"term\":\"Apply a procedure in a new situation\",\"definition\":\"Re-watching a worked example and filling in a template\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nMeasure every suggestion against the objective's verb, not against how engaging it feels. When the demand falls short of the verb, prefer modifying the suggestion up to the target over rejecting it outright, and prefer rejecting it over lowering your objective to match it.\n\nRun a suggestion through the alignment test:\n\n1. **Verb:** What cognitive demand does my objective's verb set?\n2. **Floor:** What is the least a student could do to succeed at this suggestion?\n3. **Gap:** Does that floor reach the verb, or fall short of it?\n4. **Move:** Can I add the missing rung (a reasoning stem, a justification step) while keeping the appeal?\n\nNever quietly rewrite the objective to fit an attractive activity; that is alignment drift disguised as flexibility.\n\nWork through what to do with an appealing suggestion that misses the objective by one rung, and watch where each path lands relative to the verb.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"The appealing-but-misaligned suggestion\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"Your objective is \\\"Students explain why two fractions are equivalent.\\\" The AI offers a polished timed escape room where teams race to match equivalent-fraction cards. Students would cheer for it. What do you do?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Accept it as the lesson; engagement will be high\",\"next\":\"accept\",\"feedback\":\"Engagement is not alignment. A child can win the race without ever saying why one half equals two fourths. The true demand is recognition.\"},{\"label\":\"Quietly change the objective verb to ’match’ so the activity fits\",\"next\":\"lower\",\"feedback\":\"This is silent objective-lowering: the lesson now succeeds at a goal no one set on purpose. Alignment drift disguised as flexibility.\"},{\"label\":\"Keep the appeal but add the missing rung of reasoning\",\"next\":\"modify\",\"feedback\":\"You preferred modifying the suggestion up to the verb over rejecting it, which is usually the strongest move.\"}]},\"accept\":{\"prompt\":\"On the exit ticket, students who won the race still cannot explain equivalence. What does this reveal?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The activity worked at recognition, below the explain objective; add a reasoning step\",\"next\":\"modify\",\"feedback\":\"Right. The packaging hid a recognition task; the fix is to raise the demand, not the volume.\"}]},\"lower\":{\"prompt\":\"A mentor reviewing your map asks where the objective went. How do you recover?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Restore the explain objective and modify the activity to reach it\",\"next\":\"modify\",\"feedback\":\"Never lower the objective to fit the activity. Restore the verb and raise the activity to it.\"}]},\"modify\":{\"prompt\":\"You add: after each match, students write one sentence using ’These are equivalent because...’ and a partner checks it. A third of the class reads below the card text. What else?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Add an icon model beside the words so the access barrier does not block the reasoning\",\"next\":\"done\",\"feedback\":\"You kept the cognitive demand identical while removing a decoding barrier, an adaptation rather than a reduction.\"},{\"label\":\"Give those students an easier set of cards\",\"next\":\"lower\",\"feedback\":\"That lowers the goal for some students. Change the access, not the demand.\"}]},\"done\":{\"prompt\":\"Your map now shows the verb, the true floor of the suggestion, the gap, the reasoning rung you added, and the access adjustment, all auditable.\",\"feedback\":\"Alignment is a property of the cognitive work students do: keep the appeal, add the missing rung, and never quietly lower the objective.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\nThe first misreading is to accept engagement as evidence of alignment. A loud, happy room can be working at a level far below the objective; volume is not depth. The second is to treat the AI's confidence as authority. A suggestion phrased as \"this proven activity will deepen understanding\" is still just text; the claim of depth is not the presence of depth. A polished, confident suggestion also smuggles in a commercial framing as if it were pedagogical authority, quietly shifting the pedagogical labor of deciding what counts as good teaching onto the tool. Critical edtech studies warns precisely against this burden-shift, treating a vendor's confident output as a claim to be audited rather than evidence (Selwyn, 2016; Perrotta & Selwyn, 2020). The third, subtler error is silent objective-lowering: when an activity is too fun to give up, teachers sometimes downgrade explain to match without noticing, and the lesson then succeeds at a goal no one set on purpose. The map exists to catch that drift in writing.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the alignment test in a different subject. A history objective reads: \"Students will evaluate whether a primary source is reliable.\" TeachGen@i suggests a polished interactive timeline where students drag events into chronological order. The activity is handsome and students enjoy it. Run the test. The verb is evaluate, a judgment about reliability; the floor of the timeline activity is sequencing dates, which is recall of order. The gap is two rungs. The negotiated move keeps the timeline as a context-builder but adds the real task on top: for two of the timed events, students rate the source's reliability and write the reason. The map records the timeline as kept-but-demoted (now a warm-up, not the assessment) and the reliability rating as the added rung that finally reaches evaluate.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: in the left column write the objective's verb; in the right column write the cognitive demand the suggestion actually requires. For the fractions case, the left column says \"explain (reasoning)\" and the right column says \"match (recognition).\" A strong reader sees the gap immediately and can name the smallest modification that closes it. Your map is strong when it shows the verb, the suggestion's true floor, the gap between them, and the specific rung you added, rather than a vague note that you \"made it more rigorous.\"\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong comparison map states the objective verb as the fixed test, not as background. It records the suggestion's actual cognitive floor (the least a student could do to pass), so the reviewer can see the gap you found. It shows a negotiated decision that preserves the appeal while adding the missing rung, rather than a blunt accept or reject. And it never shows the objective being quietly lowered to fit the activity. Polish is the attractive activity; evidence is the demonstrated alignment between what students will do and what you said they should learn.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy\", \"cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the test to a writing class where the objective is \"Students will revise a draft for clarity.\" TeachGen@i suggests an AI tool that rewrites student paragraphs to be clearer automatically. The activity is impressive and instant. Run the test: the verb is revise, which requires the student to do the revising; an automatic rewrite reduces the student's floor to accept, which reaches nothing on the objective. Here the gap cannot be closed by a small add-on, because the tool performs the very cognition the objective assigns to the student. The defensible decision is to repurpose the tool as a model the student critiques and then revises by hand. If you can reach that conclusion without copying the fractions logic, you understand alignment as a property of the student's cognitive work, not of the tool's output.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"An AI suggests a tool that automatically rewrites student paragraphs to be clearer, for the objective Students will revise a draft for clarity. Explain in two or three sentences why this gap cannot be closed by a small add-on, the way the card-sort gap was, and what defensible repurposing of the tool keeps the cognition with the student.\" answer=\"The card-sort gap was one rung below the verb, so adding a reasoning sentence raised it to explain. Here the auto-rewrite performs the very cognition the objective assigns to the student, so the student floor collapses to accept, which reaches nothing on revise. No small add-on fixes that. The defensible move is to repurpose the tool as a model the student critiques and then revises by hand, restoring the revising work to the learner.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/02-04-comparing-ai-suggestions-to-pedagogy-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"02.04\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 02.04. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that your map names the objective's verb, records the suggestion's true cognitive floor and the gap, and shows the rung you added (or the reason a suggestion could not be salvaged), never the objective quietly lowered to fit the activity.\n\n## References\n\n- Mishra, P., & Koehler, M. J. (2006). Technological pedagogical content knowledge: A framework for teacher knowledge ([TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- Selwyn, N. (2016). *Is technology good for education?* Polity Press.\n- Perrotta, C., & Selwyn, N. (2020). Deep learning goes to school: Toward a relational understanding of AI in education. [*Learning, Media and Technology*](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- Mecoli, S. (2013). The influence of the pedagogical content knowledge theoretical framework on research on preservice teacher education. *Journal of Education, 193*(3). https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n- Ning, Y., Zhang, C., Xu, B., Zhou, Y., & Wijaya, T. T. (2024). Teachers' AI-TPACK: Exploring the relationship between knowledge elements. *Sustainability, 16*(3), 978. https://doi.org/10.3390/su16030978\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_4_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy","stem":"TeachGen@i returns a confident, well-written suggestion to open a fractions lesson with a timed speed drill. What is the strongest basis for accepting or rejecting it?","options":["It is written clearly and sounds authoritative, so accept it.","Whether a timed speed drill serves this lesson's objective of conceptual understanding of equivalence, not just fluency.","Whether the suggestion is longer than the previous one.","Whether students will find it exciting."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Suggestions are judged against the lesson objective and an instructional principle. Fluent, confident phrasing is not pedagogical justification.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_4_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy_fluent_equals_sound","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i","cat531_supplement_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-02-04-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_4_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy","stem":"The objective is \"students will explain why two fractions are equivalent.\" The AI suggests an escape-room game where students race to match equivalent fractions. What should the teacher notice?","options":["The game is engaging, so it clearly meets the objective.","Matching equivalent fractions quickly does not require explaining why they are equivalent, so the activity does not reach the objective.","Escape rooms are always inappropriate for math.","The game should be made longer to add rigor."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An attractive activity can drift from the objective. Matching is recognition; the objective demands explanation, so the activity is decoration unless redesigned.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_4_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy_activity_over_objective","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy","evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-02-04-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_4_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","stem":"An AI suggestion works well for a typical reader, but a third of this class reads two grade levels below the dense text it assumes. What is the most defensible comparison move?","options":["Adopt it; it serves the average student well.","Test the suggestion against the class's actual reading range and add a scaffold or alternate text before using it.","Reject all AI suggestions because they cannot know the class.","Use it but tell struggling students to try harder."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A suggestion that fits the median can still fail the margins. The teacher compares it against real learner variability before adopting.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_4_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy_one_size_for_all","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-02-04-lo-03"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_4_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","stem":"On a Design Tension Studio map comparing an AI suggestion to pedagogy, which entry best documents a decision a mentor could audit?","options":["\"AI suggestion was good, kept it.\"","Tension named (AI activity's appeal vs. fit to the explain objective) paired with the decision to keep the activity but add a sentence-stem explanation step, and the reason a competing speed-drill suggestion was rejected as fluency-only.","\"Used AI, lesson done.\"","\"Suggestion was well written.\""],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A defensible map names the tension between an AI suggestion's appeal and its fit to the objective, records the negotiated change, and explains the rejection.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_4_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy_objective_lowered_to_fit_activity","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy","evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-02-04-lo-01","cat531-supplement-02-04-lo-03","cat531-supplement-02-04-lo-04"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_4_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy_fluent_equals_sound","pattern":"fluency_read_as_pedagogical_soundness","description":"Judging an AI suggestion by how confident and well-written it sounds rather than against a learning theory or standard","trigger":"teacher accepts a suggestion because it reads professionally, without checking it against the objective","feedback":"Hold each suggestion against the lesson objective and an instructional principle. Fluent phrasing is not pedagogical justification.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_4_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy_activity_over_objective","pattern":"activity_swapped_for_objective","description":"Letting an attractive AI activity replace the lesson objective instead of serving it","trigger":"teacher keeps a fun AI-suggested activity that does not assess or build the stated objective","feedback":"Trace the activity back to the objective. If it does not move students toward the target, it is decoration, however engaging.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_4_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy_one_size_for_all","pattern":"suggestion_assumed_universal","description":"Assuming an AI suggestion that suits the median student also serves the range of learners in the room","trigger":"teacher adopts a suggestion without checking it against the class's reading levels, accommodations, or language needs","feedback":"Compare the suggestion against your actual learner variability, not a generic class. A good median move can still fail the margins.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_4_comparing_ai_suggestions_to_pedagogy_objective_lowered_to_fit_activity","pattern":"objective_quietly_lowered_to_fit_activity","description":"Quietly downgrading the objective verb (explain becomes match, analyze becomes identify) so an appealing AI-suggested activity fits the lesson.","trigger":"teacher rewrites or reinterprets the objective at a lower cognitive level so the engaging activity can stay unchanged","feedback":"Hold the verb fixed and raise the activity to it; never lower the goal to fit the activity. If the activity only produces matching, add the component that forces explaining (a sentence-stem justification, a compare-and-defend step) or replace the activity.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"b486daf6d1791e871df1b7bcd00008b6f27783b5e4f2533fe0484c430bdd9ae3","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}